message.content:
After fresh rain in the empty hills,
the evening air turns autumn.
Bright moon shines through the pines;
clear springs run over stones.
Bamboo rustles — the washing women return;
lotus stirs — a fishing boat glides down.
Let the spring blossoms fade as they will;
the wanderer may well stay.

The wind blows sharp, the sky so high; the gibbons cry;
The sandbar clear, the sand bright-white, birds wheel and fly.
Boundless, the falling leaves come rustling down;
Endless, the great river rolls on toward the town.
Ten thousand miles away, in autumn grief I roam;
A hundred years, full of disease, I climb the tower alone.
Hard times, bitter regrets have thickened my frost-white temples;
Worn out, I have just set down my cup of turbid wine.

One song of new words, one cup of wine;
last year's weather, the old pavilion and terrace.
The sun sets in the west — when will it return?

Helplessly the flowers fall away;
familiar, it seems, the swallows return.
Alone I pace the small garden's fragrant path.

The wind is sharp, the sky so high, an ape's wail of grief;
The sandbar clear, the sand so white, birds wheel and turn.

Boundless, the falling leaves come rustling down;
Endless, the Yangtze rolls and surges on.

Ten thousand miles away, grieving at autumn, ever a sojourner;
A hundred years of sickness, alone I climb this terrace.

Hardship and bitter grief have thickened frost upon my temples;
Worn out, of late I have put down my cup of turbid wine.

{"translation":"Before my bed the bright moonlight falls,
I take it for frost upon the ground.
I raise my head and gaze at the bright moon;
I lower it, and think of home."}

The quiet hills after fresh rain;
the evening air turns to autumn.
Bright moon shines through the pines,
clear spring runs over the stones.
A rustle in the bamboo — washing women return;
lotus stirs — a fishing boat comes down.
Let spring's fragrance fade at will;
the nobleman may well remain.

{"translation":"Morning rain at Weicheng dampens the fine dust;
The guest house green, the willows newly bright.
Drink yet one more cup of wine, I urge you—
West of Yang Pass, no old friends remain."}

The wind is fierce, the sky is high, the gibbons cry in grief;
The sandbar clear, the sand white, birds wheeling back.
Boundless falling leaves come rustling down;
The endless Yangtze rolls on and on.
Ten thousand li from home, grieving autumn, ever a wanderer;
A hundred years of illness, alone I climb this terrace.
Hardship and bitter regret — frost thickens at my temples;
Wretched, I have just set down my cup of turbid wine.

Fierce wind, high sky, the gibbons cry in grief;
clear sandbar, white sand, birds wheel back and forth.
Leaves without end come rustling down;
the Yangtze without end comes rolling on.
Ten thousand li, autumn's sorrow, always a sojourner;
a lifetime ill, alone I climb the height.
Hardship and bitter regret have frosted my temples;
wrecked, I have just set down my cup of turbid wine.

