{"translation":"The realm lies broken; hills and rivers remain,
The city in spring, grass and trees grow deep.
Moved by the times, flowers splash with tears;
Bitter at parting, birds startle the heart.
Beacon fires have burned three months on end;
A letter from home is worth ten thousand in gold.
I scratch my white hair — it grows yet shorter,
So short it will scarcely hold a hairpin."}

A new song, a cup of wine;
Last year's weather, the old pavilion.
When will the setting sun return?

The flowers fall, beyond all help;
Seemingly familiar, the swallows return.
Alone I wander the fragrant path in the small garden.

{"translation":"One new song, one cup of wine;
last year's weather, the old pavilion and terrace.
The sun sets in the west — when will it return?

Helplessly the flowers fall away;
seemingly familiar, the swallows come back.
Alone I pace the fragrant path of the small garden."}

{"translation":"Empty hills after the fresh rain;
the evening turns to autumn.
The bright moon shines among the pines,
clear water slips over the stones.
Noise in the bamboo — the washerwomen return;
a stir in the lotus — the fishing boat drifts down.
Let spring's blossoms fade as they will —
the nobleman can well remain."}

